WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Before we take a look at how osmosis and tonicity affect

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.000
a cell, let’s review what each of these terms means.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.000
Osmosis represents the diffusion of water across a semi-permeable

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.933
membrane.

00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:22.933
The term tonicity refers to the relative solute concentration

00:00:22.933 --> 00:00:26.933
of two environments separated by a semipermeable membrane.

00:00:26.933 --> 00:00:30.933
In other words, by comparing the tonicity of the solution, 

00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:34.933
you can determine the direction in which osmosis will occur.

00:00:34.933 --> 00:00:36.233
 

00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:40.233
To demonstrate how tonicity affects a cell, 

00:00:40.233 --> 00:00:44.233
let’s place some red blood cells into a beaker containing pure water.

00:00:44.233 --> 00:00:48.233
In this case, the solute concentration is greater inside

00:00:48.233 --> 00:00:52.233
the cells, than in the surrounding water.  In other words, 

00:00:52.233 --> 00:00:56.233
the contents of the cells are hypertonic in relation to the hypotonic

00:00:56.233 --> 00:01:00.233
contents of the beaker.  Because of this, osmotic

00:01:00.233 --> 00:01:04.233
pressure results in the diffusion of water across the membrane and into the

00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:08.233
cells.  Over time, if enough water enters the cells, 

00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:12.233
the cell membranes may burst.  This is called lysis.

00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:13.200
 

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.200
Now lets place the red blood cells in a beaker containing a solution 

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.200
of salt, such as sodium chloride.  Since the contents of the beaker

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.200
beaker are hypertonic in relation to the interior of the cell, 

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.200
the water within the cell will diffuse across the membrane and into the

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.200
contents of the beaker.  This causes crenation or shrinking of

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.700
the cells.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:39.700
If the red blood cells are placed in a beaker whose contents

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.700
match the tonicity within the cells, then there is no net gain

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:47.700
or loss of water.  The environments within the beaker

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.700
and inside the cells are said to be isotonic, or the same.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:51.866
 

00:01:51.866 --> 00:01:55.866
An important thing to remember, is that osmotic pressure

00:01:55.866 --> 00:01:59.866
always causes water to move from a hypotonic environment toward

00:01:59.866 --> 00:02:03.866
a hypertonic environment.  In other words, water moves toward

00:02:03.866 --> 00:02:07.866
areas of high salt or sugar concentrations.  This simple

00:02:07.866 --> 00:02:11.866
process is used to drive the operation of our kidneys and explains

00:02:11.866 --> 00:02:15.866
some of the physiological consequences of diseases such as diabetes.

00:02:15.866 --> 00:02:18.866
 

